{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Moses wrote their goings-out according to their journeys by the mouth of HaShem.</b> Afterward he reversed the order and said, ״וְאֵלֶּה מַסְעֵיהֶם לְמוֹצָאֵיהֶם״, and it does not say in it ״by the mouth of HaShem,״ and I shall explain this in three ways. The first is this: in some journeys they traveled forward, and in some they turned back. For at first ״וְיָשֻׁבוּ וְיַחֲנוּ לִפְנֵי פִּי הַחִירֹת״ (Exodus 14:2) — Rashi explained that they went backward toward the side of Egypt; and likewise in the portion of Devarim (Deuteronomy 2:1) it is written ״וַנֵּפֶן וַנִּסַּע הַמִּדְבָּרָה״, Rashi explained that because they corrupted, they turned toward the wilderness; and likewise in the portion of Ekev (Deuteronomy 10:6) ״מִבְּאֵרֹת בְּנֵי יַעֲקָן מוֹסֵרָה״, that they went back eight journeys backward. It follows that the majority of the journeys in which they went forward and not backward, and which were by the mouth of HaShem, were called ״their goings-out according to their journeys״ — for they turned their faces from the place from which they went out, which is called ״their goings-out,״ toward ״their journeys״ which lay before them. But that which they turned backward because they corrupted through sin was not by the mouth of HaShem, and was called ״their journeys to their goings-out״ — for they went back and traveled to the place from which they had gone out, which is called ״their goings-out,״ for they turned their faces toward the side of Egypt." "The second explanation is when we examine what is meant by the statement ״וַיִּסְעוּ מֵרַעְמְסֵס״, which does not mention ״the children of Israel,״ and afterward he returned and said a second time: ״וַיִּסְעוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל מֵרַעְמְסֵס״. And furthermore, it is written here: ״מִמָּחֳרַת הַפֶּסַח יָצְאוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, and in another place it says (Exodus 16:1): ״הוֹצִיאֲךָ ה׳ מִמִּצְרַיִם לַיְלָה״. Rather, it is certain that the mixed multitude went by night in the manner of flight, and concerning them it is said (Exodus 14:5): ״וַיֻּגַּד לְמֶלֶךְ מִצְרַיִם כִּי בָרַח הָעָם״ — ״borēaḥ״ (fleeing) is not said, but rather ״bāraḥ״ (had fled), meaning they had already fled immediately upon their departure; and this does not apply to Israel, for they went with a high hand — rather, it is certain that concerning the mixed multitude it was told to him, and they are called by the name ״עָם״, but Israel went by day with a high hand. And when it says ״וַיִּסְעוּ מֵרַעְמְסֵס בַּחֹדֶשׁ הָרִאשׁוֹן״, he is speaking of the mixed multitude. And afterward he said: ״מִמָּחֳרַת הַפֶּסַח יָצְאוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּיָד רָמָה״, and afterward he said: ״וַיִּסְעוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל מֵרַעְמְסֵס״ — for the mixed multitude went first, in the manner of those who flee, and the children of Israel went after them." "And this that is said, ״וַיִּכְתֹּב מֹשֶׁה אֶת מוֹצָאֵיהֶם לְמַסְעֵיהֶם עַל פִּי ה׳״ — this is the journeying of the children of Israel whose origin is from the Holy Land; therefore they constantly turned their faces away from ״their goings-out,״ the place from which they went out, from Egypt, and the aim of their faces was ״toward their journeys,״ to come to the land that HaShem swore to their fathers. But the mixed multitude, whose origin was from Egypt and who did not go forth by the mouth of HaShem, for Moses took them out on his own initiative — concerning them it is said: ״וְאֵלֶּה מַסְעֵיהֶם לְמוֹצָאֵיהֶם״, for they always had desire and will to return to Egypt, to their origin; and as the saying goes, ״throw a stick into the air — it lands on its root״ (Genesis Rabbah 53:15) — so their faces were always turned toward the place from which they had gone out." "A third explanation is that had Israel not sinned, they would have traveled in a single journey to the Land. He said: ״וַיִּכְתֹּב מֹשֶׁה אֶת מוֹצָאֵיהֶם לְמַסְעֵיהֶם״ — for from the first departure they would have had no further departure from a camping place, for HaShem wanted to bear them on eagles' wings to the Land immediately; and because they corrupted, it was necessary to write ״וְאֵלֶּה מַסְעֵיהֶם לְמוֹצָאֵיהֶם״ — that they traveled to places whose end was to return and go out from them and to be tossed about from journey to journey, and this thing was not ״by the mouth of HaShem,״ for HaShem did not desire these."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:44:09.047409361Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}